Description: Answer frame callbacks whose buffer is never used
 Mir answers wl_surface.frame callbacks committed with a buffer when a
 compositor uses (consumes) that buffer, and it skips the buffers of fully
 covered surfaces. Umoja's live session starts the installer under the
 full-screen welcome screen: the installer's frame callback waited, GTK
 stopped drawing, and uncovering it did not bring a composite that used
 the waiting buffer. The installer kept working (a click moved it to the
 next page) but the screen never changed until a VT switch redrew
 everything.
 .
 Callbacks still waiting 0.5 s after their commit (checked by a second
 FrameExecutor with a 500 ms delay, so 0.5-1 s) are now answered anyway,
 as other compositors throttle hidden surfaces instead of stalling them. A
 covered GTK window redraws at about 1 Hz (34% CPU instead of 333% for the
 installer's animation on llvmpipe in a VM); a visible one has its buffer
 used within a frame and never reaches the fallback.
Author: Umoja developers
Forwarded: no

